We begin by expanding the left side of the equation:
\begin{align*}
\left(5x^2-3x+\frac{7}{3}\right)(ax^2+bx+c) &= 5ax^4 + 5bx^3 + 5cx^2 - 3ax^3 - 3bx^2 - 3cx + \frac{7}{3}ax^2 + \frac{7}{3}bx + \frac{7}{3}c \\
&= 5ax^4 + 2bx^3 + \left(5c - 3b + \frac{7}{3}a\right)x^2 + \left(\frac{7}{3}b - 3c\right)x + \frac{7}{3}c
\end{align*}
Now, we compare the coefficients of the expanded form with the right side of the equation:
\begin{align*}
5a &= 15 \\
2b &= -14 \\
5c - 3b + \frac{7}{3}a &= 20 \\
\frac{7}{3}b - 3c &= -\frac{25}{3} \\
\frac{7}{3}c &= \frac{14}{3}
\end{align*}
Solving the first equation for $a$ gives $a = 3$. Substituting $a = 3$ into the second equation gives $2b = -14$, so $b = -7$. Substituting $b = -7$ into the third equation gives $5c - 3(-7) + \frac{7}{3}(3) = 20$, so $5c + 21 + 7 = 20$, which gives $5c = -8$, and finally, $c = -\frac{8}{5}$.
Therefore, the constant $b$ is $\boxed{-7}$.The answer is: -7
